data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**A1 · Keep carts in Redis or Postgres?**\n\n**The short version:** Carts have outgrown the 4 KB session cookie, so they need to live on the server. The question is which server-side store to use. I'd pick Postgres.\n\n**A concrete example:** A shopper adds 30 items across two days, then opens the site on their phone. The cart has to still be there, with the right prices and quantities. The cookie can't hold that, so the server keeps it and the browser holds only a small ID.\n\n**Option a1: Redis**\n- Very fast reads and writes, and keys can expire on their own, which suits short-lived sessions.\n- Leads to: a new service to run, monitor, back up, and secure. Unless Redis is configured for persistence, a restart or eviction can silently drop carts. A lost cart is a lost sale.\n- Joining cart data with products, prices, or stock is awkward, because the data lives in a separate store.\n\n**Option a2: Postgres (recommended by the main agent)**\n- Carts are durable and survive restarts, and they stay findable days later or across devices.\n- You can query them alongside products and inventory in one place, and use transactions when checking out.\n- Leads to: a bit more write load on the database than Redis. That's usually fine at typical cart volumes, and you can add a cache later if it isn't.\n- Assumes you already run Postgres. If you don't, that adds the same operational cost Redis would.\n\n**My recommendation:** Postgres. Carts are business data that should be kept, and it adds no new infrastructure if you already use Postgres. Choose Redis only if carts are truly disposable (say, expire within an hour) and you accept losing them, or if you already run Redis and not Postgres.\n\n**What I could verify:** The project checkout has one commit (`init`) and no tracked files, so I couldn't confirm which databases the codebase already uses. The recommendation depends on that. If the main agent can confirm it's already running Postgres, Postgres is the clear choice.\n\nA1 is still open. Choose a1 (Redis) or a2 (Postgres) on the ask, or tell me here if you want more detail on either option."}}

